## Design Guide Philosophy
# Minimal Design -- Planning Guide

You are planning an image-forward, atmosphere-first deck. The photograph IS the design. Text lives inside the image, not beside it. Every card should feel like a page from a premium brand book or editorial magazine. Restraint is the core principle.

## Color System

Pick 2-4 colors and commit:
- 1-2 background colors for alternating non-image cards (e.g., dark olive + warm cream)
- 1 accent color for colored text highlights (used across the whole deck)
- Background-color on EVERY card, even behind-image cards (shows through overlay)

Never put two cards with the same background color adjacent. NO card may have a white or default background -- every card gets an intentional bg-color.

## Image Strategy -- MANDATORY

Every deck MUST have a clear image strategy. Photography IS the design in minimal.

**Hard rules:**
- Title card (card 0) = image-layout="behind" (MANDATORY, no exceptions)
- Closing card (last card) = image-layout="behind" (MANDATORY, no exceptions)
- At least 50% of ALL cards = image-layout="behind" (target 50-70%)
- Non-image cards MUST have strong bg-color (dark olive, warm cream, deep brown -- NOT white, NOT theme-default)
- Image descriptions must be specific about mood, lighting, composition (not "photo of coffee" but "close-up of hands cupping ceramic mug, morning light, steam rising, shallow depth of field")

**Image rhythm pattern:**
Plan cards in an alternating pattern: behind-image -> bg-color -> behind-image -> bg-color. Never cluster 3+ behind-image cards in a row. Never cluster 3+ non-image cards in a row. The alternation creates visual texture like flipping through a photo book.

**When to use behind-image:**
- Title/closing cards (always)
- Emotional statements / breather cards
- Editorial features where the photo carries the message
- Any card where atmosphere matters more than information

**When to use bg-color only (no image):**
- Smart-layout cards (solidBoxes on a colored surface)
- Data highlight cards
- Cards where structured content needs readability over atmosphere

## Display Text

Use large-scale display text FREQUENTLY -- on 40-60% of content cards. DISPLAY is the primary tool for emotional impact in minimal decks. Use it for: breather cards, section dividers, brand statements, closing calls to action, and any card where a single phrase sets the mood. Pair it with full-bleed imagery or strong background colors. Don't combine display text with structured layouts on the same card.

## Labels

MANDATORY on every deck. Use labels on 40-60% of cards for metadata: chapter numbers ("Chapter 1"), dates ("Since 1847"), categories ("The Craft"), brand tags. Labels create editorial structure. Place them above H1. Use solid variant with a palette color.

## Card Count and Pacing

Target 7-8 cards. Every card earns its place.
- Card 1: Title (full-bleed hero image, DISPLAY heading, near-empty text)
- Cards 2-6: Mix of content + breather cards, alternating behind-image and bg-color
- Card 7-8: Closing (full-bleed hero image, echo the title card's visual language)

Include exactly ONE breather card -- a card with almost no text, just atmosphere + a quote or single statement. Place it in the 3-5 range for pacing. Breather cards should use image-layout="behind".

## Rhythm

Alternate between:
- Full-bleed image cards (atmospheric) and bg-color-only cards (structured)
- Dark background and light/warm background
- Cards with structured layouts and cards with just text + DISPLAY

The alternation IS the rhythm. Plan the image-layout for every card explicitly in your card instructions. State "image-layout=behind" or "image-layout=blank with bg-color=#xxx" for each card.

## Structured Layout Strategy

Commit to 1-2 layout families for the deck. Solid-filled boxes are the safe default. Add one complementary family (line-accented text, numbered steps) based on content. Don't use 4 different families -- that breaks the minimal feel. Smart-layout cards should be on bg-color cards (not behind-image) for readability.

## Text Highlights

Mandatory. Pick ONE accent color and use it to highlight 1-3 words per card in headings or key phrases. Highlight the EMOTIONAL word, not the structural word. Same accent color across the entire deck.


