You receive a spatial description and card content. Produce a single <SECTION> block.

CRITICAL RULES:
- Output ONLY a single <SECTION> block. No explanation, no markdown fences.
- Use ONLY valid GML tags: SECTION, DIV, H1, H2, H3, H4, P, UL, OL, LI, B, I, U, S, COLUMNS, SMART-LAYOUT, SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM, DISPLAY, LABELS, LABEL, ICON, IMG, ASIDE, BLOCKQUOTE, TABLE, TR, TD, TH, CHART, GRAPH, DIAGRAM, INFOGRAPHIC, GALLERY, BUTTONS, BUTTON, HR, PRE, FORMULA, MATH, SPAN, A, MARK, BR, CODE
- NEVER invent tags. No <COLUMN>, <HEADING>, <TEXT>, <CARD>, <ITEM>, or any non-GML tag.
- SECTION requires image-layout attribute (blank, left, right, top, or behind)
- SECTION supports vertical-align attribute (start, center, or end)
- If image-layout is blank: SECTION contains only <DIV class="body">
- If image-layout is left/right/top/behind: SECTION contains <IMG class="accent-image"> BEFORE <DIV class="body">
- COLUMNS contains <DIV> elements. Every column MUST have content — never create empty <DIV></DIV> columns.
- If image-layout is left/right, the image is at the section level. Do NOT create COLUMNS to reserve space for the image. Use full-width content instead.
- data-vertical-align on DIV inside COLUMNS: start, center, or end
- data-background-color on at most ONE DIV inside COLUMNS
- SMART-LAYOUT supports horizontalAlign attribute: left, center, or right. Apply this when alignment is specified.
- Text alignment: style="text-align: left" or style="text-align: right" on text blocks
- If spatial description says "left-aligned" or "right-aligned", apply to ALL text blocks AND set horizontalAlign on any SMART-LAYOUT tags
- When the spatial description names a content type (metrics display, sequential process, parallel items), use the appropriate SMART-LAYOUT variant from the guide's block rules. "metrics display" → stats variant. "sequential process" → topLineText variant. "parallel items" → outlineBoxes variant.

## Design Guide — Block Rules
import type { BlocksConfig } from '../schema'

export const minimalBlocks: BlocksConfig = {
  section: {
    imageLayoutPreferences: {
      preferred: ['behind'],
      allowed: ['blank', 'left', 'right'],
      avoided: ['top'],
    },
    verticalAlignDefault: 'start',
    backgroundColorAllowed: true,
    // CRITICAL: image-layout="behind" is STRONGLY preferred. 50-70% of cards should be behind-image.
    // Title card and closing card MUST be behind-image. "blank" only for smart-layout or structured cards.
    // Every card MUST have background-color regardless of image-layout.
  },

  columns: {
    allowed: true,
    preferredSplits: ['35,65', '40,60', '65,35', '50,50'],
    maxColumns: 2,
    guidance: `Prefer asymmetric splits — [35,65] or [65,35]. Narrow column = text, wide = image.
Use data-vertical-align="center" on column divs.
data-background-color on a column div creates a colored panel effect.
Three-column layouts extremely rare — max once per deck, only for image grids.
Never use equal-width columns for text-only content.`,
  },

  text: {
    roleMapping: {
      heroMoment: 'DISPLAY:md',
      cardTitle: 'H1',
      supporting: 'H4',
      metadata: 'LABEL:solid',
    },
    emphasisMethod: 'text-color-span',
    textColorSpans: {
      frequency: 'every-card',
      maxWordsPerSpan: 3,
      // CRITICAL: Every card MUST have at least one <span text-color="..."> wrapping
      // 1-3 words in its heading. Pick the emotional word. Same accent color deck-wide.
      // On dark backgrounds, also use text-color="#FFFFFF" on body text for readability.
    },
    defaultTextAlign: 'left',
  },

  smartLayout: {
    allowed: true,
    preferredVariants: [
      'solidBoxes',
      'topLineText',
      'sideLineText',
      'processSteps',
      'minimalTimeline',
      'iconsText',
      'imagesText',
      'steps',
    ],
    avoidedVariants: [
      'outlineBoxes',
      'arrowBullets',
      'circleStats',
      'dotGridStats',
      'starStats',
      'speechBubbles',
      'quoteBoxes',
      'flower',
    ],
    maxFamiliesPerDeck: 1,
    maxItemsPerLayout: 3,
    variantPreferenceOrder: {
      boxes: ['solidBoxes'],
      text: ['topLineText', 'sideLineText'],
    },
    guidance: `Commit to 1-2 variant families per deck. solidBoxes is the default workhorse.
Each item: H4 heading 2-5 words, P description 1 sentence max 15 words.
MANDATORY: Use "color" attr on EVERY smart-layout-item to tie into palette.
Each item should be a slightly different shade for tonal variation.
Colored smart layout items are THE defining minimal trait — never leave items uncolored.
No more than 4-5 cards in a deck should have smart layouts.`,
    variantGuidance: {
      solidBoxes: `The workhorse. 2-3 items with H4 + 1 sentence. Use color attr
on each item for tonal variation within the palette.`,
      topLineText: `Elegant, ultra-minimal. Good for luxury/editorial decks.
Horizontal orientation preferred.`,
      sideLineText: `Vertical line accent. Use inside columns when smart layout
needs to fit a narrow container.`,
      processSteps: `For 3-step processes. Always numbered=true.
One sentence descriptions. Good with behind image if steps are simple.`,
    },
  },

  display: {
    allowed: true,
    guidance: `MANDATORY on 40-60% of cards. DISPLAY is the primary typographic tool in minimal.
Good for: title cards, quote/breather cards, section dividers, brand statements, CTAs, closing sign-offs.
Bad for: cards with smart layouts, cards with more than 2 sentences.
Use DISPLAY as the MAIN heading on any card that aims for emotional impact.
Pair with a label above for editorial structure. Always pair with image-layout="behind" or strong background-color.
DISPLAY + behind-image is the signature minimal combination — cinematic text over photography.`,
  },

  aside: {
